BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iac-bc-ai-builders-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260821
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260822
SUMMARY:Deadline: AI Builders Fellowship 2026 (Internet Archive Canada x BC
  + AI)
DESCRIPTION:A $5\,000 fellowship to spend six weeks turning a very large Ca
 nadian public archive into something new. THE MATERIAL: Internet Archive C
 anada's Government Publications collection\, part of the Democracy's Libra
 ry project\, holding over 104\,000 text items\, roughly 22.1 million scann
 ed pages and about 48.25 TB of Canadian public-record material\, browsable
  at archive.org/details/governmentpublications. WHAT TO BUILD: deliberatel
 y open-ended. The call names a search tool that answers a real question wi
 th page-level citations\, a timeline tracing one policy across decades of 
 government language\, and a sonic or visual piece built from archival mate
 rial\, but explicitly invites work nobody has thought of yet. The requirem
 ents are that it is useful\, that it is imaginative\, and that the code is
  open source when it is done. ORIGIN: the fellowship came out of conversat
 ions between BC + AI's Kris Kruger and IAC executive director Andrea Mills
  about treating a public archive as material for new work rather than only
  something to preserve. IAC brings the archive\, BC + AI brings a communit
 y of builders used to working in public. ELIGIBILITY: anyone\, anywhere in
  the world\; one builder is selected. APPLICATION: a specific project desc
 ription grounded in the collection\, a phase-by-phase work plan for the si
 x weeks\, and a success metric\, submitted at bc-ai.ca/fellowship/apply. K
 EY DATES: applications close 21 August 2026 at 11:59pm Pacific\; fellow se
 lected by 31 August\; build runs 1 September to mid-October\; showcase at 
 Futureproof Festival in Vancouver on 28 October 2026 if the project is rea
 dy.\n\nOrganisation: Internet Archive Canada (IAC) in partnership with BC 
 + AI\nLocation: Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional 
 in-person showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 Oct
 ober 2026 if the project is ready.\nAward: $5\,000 award for one selected 
 builder. No application fee.\nDuration: Six-week build\, 1 September to mi
 d-October 2026\, remote\, with support from BC + AI throughout\n\nMore: ht
 tps://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
URL:https://bc-ai.ca/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, open to builders anywhere in the world. Optional in-perso
 n showcase at Futureproof Festival in Vancouver\, Canada on 28 October 202
 6 if the project is ready.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:inato-gallery-touch-some-grass-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Inato Gallery: Touch Some Grass (open-call poster exhibit
 ion)
DESCRIPTION:Touch Some Grass is an open-call poster exhibition responding t
 o the growing presence of AI-generated imagery and its impact on creative 
 labour\, authorship and visual culture. At a time when images can be produ
 ced instantly and consumed just as quickly\, the exhibition reclaims the p
 oster as a space for ideas\, intention and public conversation\, inviting 
 artists to make work that questions\, challenges or reflects on this chang
 ing landscape. As the organisers put it\, the question is no longer whethe
 r machines can make images\, they can\; the question is what kind of world
  we build when speed matters more than thought. NOTE: this call offers exh
 ibition and visibility only\, with no artist fee or stipend. SUBMISSION GU
 IDELINES: artwork size A3 (297 x 420 mm)\, portrait orientation\, 300 DPI\
 , CMYK recommended\, print-quality PDF preferred\, maximum file size 1 GB\
 , one submission per artist. CAP: participation is limited to 60 artists a
 nd the submission form closes automatically once all slots are filled\, so
  the effective deadline may fall well before 31 August 2026 (10 of 60 slot
 s were taken at the time of listing). Inato Gallery is an artist-run space
  in Iligan City\, Philippines\, which began as a solo street exhibition by
  KaraTula and now runs concept-driven solo and group shows\, open mics\, t
 alks and workshops built on openness\, participation and the belief that a
 rt should exist where people already are.\n\nOrganisation: Inato Gallery\n
 Location: Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or tr
 avel is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-
 run space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stat
 ed.\nAward: No artist fee\, stipend or production budget. This is an exhib
 ition opportunity: selected posters are shown in Inato Gallery's Touch Som
 e Grass exhibition. No submission fee is stated in the call.\nDuration: On
 e-off poster exhibition. Exhibition dates are not announced yet.\n\nMore: 
 https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.inatogallery.com/open-call?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Submissions are digital (A3 portrait PDF) and no residency or trav
 el is required\; the exhibition is mounted by Inato Gallery\, an artist-ru
 n space in Iligan City\, Philippines. No nationality restriction is stated
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-episodic-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260825
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Institute: Episodic Program and Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Sundance Institute Episodic Program is an entry point for e
 merging to mid-career writers and creators working across all scripted ser
 ies platforms\, combining a lab experience with customised support to move
  projects and careers forward. The Episodic Lab lets writers workshop an o
 riginal pilot and develop their writing and pitching skills: working with 
 showrunners\, producers and executives\, fellows take part in one-on-one s
 tory meetings\, pitching sessions\, craft workshops and writers rooms. Up 
 to ten applicants are selected\, either for the in-person Lab (preceded by
  virtual workshops) or for a fully virtual Intensive with a creative advis
 or. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or older\; the script must be the a
 pplicant's original creation or adapted from optioned source material and 
 written in English\; the draft must be revised or new\, not already produc
 ed\, not sold to a third party and not distributed online\; and applicants
  cannot have previously had an episodic project (pilot or pitch) produced 
 by a studio or network. Open to international and U.S. participants\, subj
 ect to travel guidelines. Up to two writers per project may participate\, 
 with one named as primary applicant. APPLICATION: submitted via FilmFreewa
 y\, including writer bios (250 words each)\, a series logline (50 words)\,
  pilot synopsis (500 words)\, one-page series overview (500 words)\, perso
 nal statement (250 words)\, community connection\, thematic statement (250
  words)\, characters and core relationships (250 words)\, episodic vision\
 , optional video samples (up to three links\, 10 minutes total)\, and the 
 full pilot script as a PDF in standard screenplay format. Demographic ques
 tions are optional and do not affect consideration. Candidates may submit 
 up to three applications\, with a separate application and USD 50 fee for 
 each project. Submissions opened 13 July 2026 and close 25 August 2026 at 
 6:00 p.m. PT. Finalists are notified by email through FilmFreeway around m
 id to late March 2027 (FilmFreeway lists 31 March 2027)\, and the list of 
 selected writers and projects is published at sundance.org/epi after 15 Ap
 ril 2027. Finalists must return a Fellow Agreement\, a Participation Agree
 ment and any other required documents within five days. Questions: episodi
 cprogram@sundance.org.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Institute\nLocation: Open
  to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes place in the At
 lanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive track is fully o
 nline.\nAward: No stipend or honorarium for participation. For the in-pers
 on Lab\, Sundance covers round-trip basic economy airfare from the airport
  nearest the fellow's residence\, shared housing with a private room and b
 ath\, and ground transport to and from the airport (fellows within 100 mil
 es of the Lab get ground transport only). Meals\, in-room charges\, travel
  insurance\, luggage fees and taxes are the fellow's own cost. One selecte
 d project also receives a separate monetary grant from the Alfred P. Sloan
  Foundation for a series with an illuminating emphasis on science\, includ
 ing medicine\, energy\, technology or invention. APPLICATION FEE: USD 50 n
 onrefundable processing fee per project submitted.\nDuration: In-person La
 b in Spring 2027 (event dates 15 to 20 April 2027) preceded by virtual wor
 kshops\; the Intensive track convenes virtually in Summer 2027.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes pla
 ce in the Atlanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive trac
 k is fully online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-collab-merrell-storytelling-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260818
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260819
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Collab x Merrell: Brand-Supported Storytelling P
 rogram 2026
DESCRIPTION:Sundance Collab and Merrell are selecting five filmmakers from 
 around the world to each make an original 60 to 90 second vertical film fo
 r social platforms\, built around Merrell's 'It Starts Outside' brand plat
 form. The brief is not a commercial: it asks for a complete\, emotionally 
 resonant human story in which the outside acts as a catalyst for change\, 
 exploring how stepping outside is an antidote to increasingly screen-locke
 d\, indoor lives. Stories should reflect transformation (a moment of reset
 \, reconnection or personal shift)\, connection (belonging\, community\, r
 enewed relationship) and human truth (an observed\, emotionally grounded m
 oment). The strongest entries show what changed in a person because they w
 ent outside\, emotionally\, physically\, mentally\, creatively\, spiritual
 ly or psychologically\, rather than a final achievement. Selected filmmake
 rs enter a three-day online intensive in September on collaborating with a
  brand\, then receive USD 20\,000 to produce and deliver their greenlit co
 ncept\, cutdowns (:06\, :15\, :30) and stills between October and December
 \, with scheduled check-ins. One participant also receives a covered hotel
  stay to join the team at the Sundance Film Festival in January 2027. APPL
 ICATION: one entry per person\; all submitted materials must be created by
  the submitter\; all answers and materials must be in English or carry Eng
 lish subtitles. The core submission is a written response of up to 500 wor
 ds on how you would approach the brief. CRITICAL GUIDELINES: design for ve
 rtical with a strong hook in the first five seconds\; prioritise human\, o
 bservational storytelling over polished commercial aesthetics\; feature re
 al people and authentic moments\; treat the outside as context rather than
  the hero\; favour everyday\, intimate moments over epic outcomes\; avoid 
 overt brand narration\; no competitor products (Merrell product is provide
 d but must not be the focus)\; no AI-generated content in consumer-facing 
 materials. Selection criteria are originality\, emotional arc\, a distinct
  point of view and cultural relevance. TIMELINE: submissions opened 28 Jul
 y 2026\; deadline 11:59 p.m. PT on Tuesday 18 August 2026 (08:59 CEST on 1
 9 August)\; selected artists notified by 16 September 2026. An Insider Ses
 sion with Merrell Creative Director Alex Ho and Sundance Collab Director P
 atty West runs on 11 August. Sundance Collab provides accommodations and s
 upport services for participants with disabilities\; email accessibility@s
 undance.org to arrange services.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Collab (Sundanc
 e Institute) in collaboration with Merrell\nLocation: Online program\, ope
 n to filmmakers worldwide. Production takes place wherever the filmmaker i
 s based. One participant receives a covered hotel stay at the Sundance Fil
 m Festival in Park City\, Utah\, USA in January 2027.\nAward: USD 20\,000 
 production budget per selected filmmaker to make and deliver the greenlit 
 concept\, cutdowns (:06\, :15\, :30) and stills. Includes an online brand-
 supported storytelling program\, and a covered hotel stay for one particip
 ant to attend the Sundance Film Festival in January 2027. No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Program runs September to December 2026\, with an online in
 tensive 30 September to 2 October 2026\, production in October\, post-prod
 uction in November and delivery by 14 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://coll
 ab.sundance.org/catalog/Merrell-Brand-Supported-Storytelling-Program?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collab.sundance.org/catalog/Merrell-Brand-Supported-Storytellin
 g-Program?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online program\, open to filmmakers worldwide. Production takes pl
 ace wherever the filmmaker is based. One participant receives a covered ho
 tel stay at the Sundance Film Festival in Park City\, Utah\, USA in Januar
 y 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jugaad-absolute-improvisation-sac-mama-magnet-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260905
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260906
SUMMARY:Deadline: JUGAAD: Absolute Improvisation\, open call for printed ma
 tter and small-format works (Amsterdam)
DESCRIPTION:Open call from SAC Art Amsterdam and Mama Magnet for a printed 
 matter fair themed around jugaad: improvised\, resourceful solutions built
  from what is on hand (from the Sanskrit yuj\, 'to join'\, via Hindi jugar
 \, famously embodied by post-independence Punjabi vehicles pieced together
  from scrap metal and pump engines). The curators want work that engages t
 he spirit of jugaad either as subject (everyday intelligences of getting b
 y\, lateral logics of adaptation\, unlikely workarounds\, reinvention born
  of necessity) or in how it was made (found or repurposed materials\, adap
 ted processes\, structures built to solve a specific constraint). Work tha
 t came from adjustment rather than access\, problem-solving rather than bu
 dget. WHAT: zines\, artist books\, comics\, risograph or photocopied print
 s\, posters\, foldables\, instruction manuals\, packaging and other small-
 format works\; if it can travel in a suitcase it is probably in scope. No 
 framed work or anything needing gallery-standard installation. Gen-AI imag
 es and text are not accepted. WHO: artists\, designers\, writers and colle
 ctives whose practice genuinely engages with the theme\; if unsure\, reach
  out and explain in a line or two. Selected work must reach the Netherland
 s by end of September 2026. TERMS: selections made on a rolling basis 5 Au
 gust to 5 September\; selected work is sold at the fair (9-10 October\, Am
 sterdam) on mutually agreeable commission terms\, with payment within 30 d
 ays of each sale and unsold work returned after the consignment period. HO
 W TO APPLY: email info@mamamagnet.org with a CC to art@sacartamsterdam.com
 \, subject line 'JUGAAD - [Your Contribution]'\, by 5 September 2026\; ear
 lier is better given rolling selection.\n\nOrganisation: SAC Art Amsterdam
  and Mama Magnet\nLocation: Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterda
 m. Open internationally\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherl
 ands by the end of September 2026 at the artist's expense.\nAward: No appl
 ication fee. Selected work is sold at the printed matter fair on mutually 
 agreeable terms regarding commission\, duration and number of editions\; a
 rtists are remunerated within 30 days of each sale. Unsold works are retur
 ned at the end of the agreed consignment period.\nDuration: Rolling select
 ion 5 August to 5 September 2026\; fair 9-10 October 2026\n\nMore: https:/
 /sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://sacartamsterdam.com/upcoming-programmes/?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Fair takes place 9-10 October 2026 in Amsterdam. Open internationa
 lly\, but selected work must be shipped to the Netherlands by the end of S
 eptember 2026 at the artist's expense.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ars-biologica-artxscience-residency-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.
 io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ars Biologica ArtXScience Residency 2027-2028 (Ars Electr
 onica x Ceske Budejovice ECoC 2028)
DESCRIPTION:A fully funded art-science residency from Ars Electronica and C
 eske Budejovice\, European Capital of Culture 2028\, exploring how art and
  science can collaborate on urgent environmental challenges: climate emerg
 ence\, sustainable agriculture\, geoengineering\, permaculture\, water res
 ource management\, and the broader ecological impacts on local communities
 . Selected residents work in direct collaboration with local scientists in
  Ceske Budejovice. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, designers\, creatives and arti
 st collectives with more than 5 years of professional creative practice an
 d documented interdisciplinary work\; experience in community engagement a
 nd science collaborations is essential\; applicants must be 18+\, of any n
 ationality\, and legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. WHAT YOU G
 ET: a EUR 24\,000 package (EUR 6\,000 artist fee\, up to EUR 15\,000 produ
 ction\, up to EUR 3\,000 travel and subsistence) plus one month of housing
  covered separately\; two winners are selected. STRUCTURE: the residency r
 uns January 2027 to April 2028\, with a required on-site stay of up to one
  month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/September 2027\
 , and a March 2028 stay for festival installation\, exhibition and deinsta
 llation. HOW TO APPLY: online form with project title\, a written proposal
  of max 250 words\, a scientific direction statement (max 100 words)\, art
 istic vision (max 100 words)\, community connection plan (max 100 words)\,
  a super short pitch (3 to 30 words)\, 3 to 7 images\, an optional 1-minut
 e video\, a portfolio link and contact information. Two-round selection (o
 nline application then interview)\; winners notified by November 2026. Dea
 dline 28 September 2026 at midnight CET.\n\nOrganisation: Ars Electronica 
 and Ceske Budejovice - European Capital of Culture 2028\nLocation: Ceske B
 udejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any nationality aged 18+
  who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Required on-site s
 tays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional stay in August/
 September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibition and deinstal
 lation.\nAward: EUR 24\,000 total package per winner: EUR 6\,000 artist fe
 e\, up to EUR 15\,000 production budget\, and up to EUR 3\,000 for travel\
 , accommodation and subsistence\, plus a separate budget covering one mont
 h of housing in Ceske Budejovice. Two positions available. Free to apply.\
 nDuration: January 2027 to April 2028\, with on-site blocks in early 2027 
 and March 2028\n\nMore: https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars
 -biologica-art-science-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://ars.electronica.art/export/en/ecoc2028-ars-biologica-art-scienc
 e-residency/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Ceske Budejovice\, Czech Republic. Open to applicants of any natio
 nality aged 18+ who are legally eligible to work and travel in the EU. Req
 uired on-site stays: up to one month in February/March 2027\, an optional 
 stay in August/September 2027\, and March 2028 for installation\, exhibiti
 on and deinstallation.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
 einecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/short-term?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/beinecke/researchers/fellowships/shor
 t-term?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Onsite at Yale Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connectic
 ut\, USA. Open to academic and independent scholars locally and globally\,
  regardless of institutional association.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:knowcase-sonar-cotec-open-call-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260905T151547Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260907
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:Deadline: KNOWCASE Open Call 2027: permanent digital-culture instal
 lation in Madrid (Sonar+D x Fundacion Cotec)
DESCRIPTION:Fundacion Cotec (a Spanish innovation-focused non-profit) and S
 onar+D\, building on an initial experiment at Sonar 2026\, invite proposal
 s for a long-term installation at KNOWCASE\, the street-level showcase of 
 Cotec's Madrid headquarters in Chamberi\, visible to the public 24 hours a
  day. THEME: digital culture\, meaning projects that combine knowledge\, r
 esearch\, science and data with art\, technology and social impact. Cotec'
 s stated priorities are the big transitions (analogue to digital\, linear 
 to circular economy\, tangible to intangible) plus innovation ecosystems a
 nd R&D&I\, the climate crisis\, the future of work and learning\, social d
 ivides\, citizen participation and democratic health. WHAT YOU GET: a budg
 et of up to EUR 100\,000 plus VAT covering everything (design\, production
 \, installation\, maintenance\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, acc
 ommodation and insurance)\, and a minimum 24-month public display from Feb
 ruary 2027. WHO CAN APPLY: artists\, collectives\, designers\, technologis
 ts\, universities\, research centres\, startups\, businesses\, creative ag
 encies\, producers\, cultural institutions and multidisciplinary teams\, i
 nternationally\; applicants must demonstrate solid experience and a releva
 nt portfolio. CONSTRAINTS: the proposal must be original and never previou
 sly exhibited\, must include participative or interactive elements\, must 
 encourage critical thinking\, and must be technically and financially viab
 le for the full 24+ month exhibition period (durability and maintenance ar
 e central selection criteria). Selection weighs conceptual quality highest
  (40 percent)\, followed by public interactivity (25 percent). HOW TO APPL
 Y: one PDF of maximum 15 pages containing summary\, concept\, technical pr
 oposal\, visuals\, budget\, timeline\, team profile\, portfolio and declar
 ation\, in Spanish\, another official language of Spain\, or English\, via
  the application portal linked from the terms and conditions. Results anno
 unced 14 October 2026. Deadline 7 September 2026 at midnight CEST.\n\nOrga
 nisation: Fundacion Cotec and Sonar+D\nLocation: Installation at the KNOWC
 ASE storefront of Fundacion Cotec's headquarters in Chamberi\, Madrid\, Sp
 ain\, a street-level venue visible 24/7. The call is international\; appli
 cations accepted in Spanish\, other official languages of Spain\, or Engli
 sh.\nAward: Up to EUR 100\,000 (plus VAT)\, all-inclusive: conceptualisati
 on\, design\, technical development\, production\, installation\, maintena
 nce\, documentation\, artist fees\, travel\, accommodation and insurance.\
 nDuration: Installation displayed for a minimum of 24 months\, starting Fe
 bruary 2027\n\nMore: https://sonar.es/en/news/knowcase-open-call-2027?utm_
